problems.problem:
A $1\times k$ board is called guayaco if:
• each unit square is painted with exactly one of $k$ available colors;
• if $\gcd(i,k)>1$, the $i$th unit square is painted with the same color as the $(i-1)$th unit square;
• if $\gcd(i, k)=1$, the $i$th unit square is painted with the same color as the $(k-i)$th unit square.
Sebastian chooses a positive integer $a$ and calculates the number of guayaco $1\times a$ boards. After that, David chooses a positive integer $b$ and calculates the number of guayaco $1\times b$ boards. David wins if these two numbers are equal; otherwise Sebastian wins. Find all pairs $(a,b)$ for which David wins.
